1
00:00:01,050 --> 00:00:04,120
Imagine you are floating
in cold, dark water.

2
00:00:04,610 --> 00:00:07,750
Twenty metres away, a black fin rises.

3
00:00:08,450 --> 00:00:09,450
Then another.

4
00:00:09,890 --> 00:00:11,190
Then five more.

5
00:00:13,876 --> 00:00:15,353
You are now sharing the sea with

6
00:00:15,403 --> 00:00:17,456
the most successful
predator on the planet.

7
00:00:18,169 --> 00:00:21,739
This animal hunts great white
sharks and tears out their livers.

8
00:00:22,349 --> 00:00:26,399
It drowns blue whales, the largest
animals that have ever lived.

9
00:00:26,839 --> 00:00:29,679
It makes waves to wash seals off the ice.

10
00:00:30,209 --> 00:00:34,389
It throws itself onto beaches to
snatch sea lions from the shore.

11
00:00:35,078 --> 00:00:37,248
Nothing in the ocean is safe from it.

12
00:00:38,885 --> 00:00:41,675
Except, it seems, you.

13
00:00:45,295 --> 00:00:49,333
In the wild, across all of recorded
history, there is not a single

14
00:00:49,383 --> 00:00:51,985
confirmed case of an orca killing a human.

15
00:00:54,642 --> 00:00:58,312
The ocean's deadliest predator
refuses to treat us as prey.

16
00:00:58,912 --> 00:01:00,373
And the reason why may be one

17
00:01:00,423 --> 00:01:02,872
of the strangest stories
in the animal kingdom.

18
00:01:09,037 --> 00:01:12,083
To understand how strange
that is, we first

19
00:01:12,133 --> 00:01:15,157
have to understand what an orca really is.

20
00:01:15,864 --> 00:01:17,429
The orca, or killer whale, is not really

21
00:01:17,479 --> 00:01:19,174
a whale in the way we usually picture one.

22
00:01:19,714 --> 00:01:22,864
It is the largest member of
the oceanic dolphin family.

23
00:01:23,482 --> 00:01:26,972
It can grow to nearly ten metres
long and weigh up to ten tonnes.

24
00:01:27,622 --> 00:01:31,802
Females can live an estimated
eighty to ninety years.

25
00:01:32,282 --> 00:01:33,312
Males, fifty to sixty.

26
00:01:34,011 --> 00:01:38,991
Orcas live in every ocean on Earth,
from the polar ice to the tropics.

27
00:01:39,591 --> 00:01:42,105
They hunt in coordinated
family groups, and

28
00:01:42,155 --> 00:01:44,421
they have no natural predators at all.

29
00:01:45,104 --> 00:01:47,558
Scientists estimate that there are around

30
00:01:47,608 --> 00:01:50,234
fifty thousand orcas
in the world's oceans.

31
00:01:50,844 --> 00:01:55,482
Fifty thousand of the most capable
predators that have ever lived, sharing

32
00:01:55,532 --> 00:01:57,636
the sea with millions of swimmers,

33
00:01:57,686 --> 00:02:00,284
surfers, divers and fishermen every year.

34
00:02:00,956 --> 00:02:06,906
Fish, squid, seals, sea lions, dolphins,
sharks, even the great whales.

35
00:02:07,096 --> 00:02:13,876
If it lives in the sea, some orca,
somewhere, has learned to kill it.

36
00:02:14,565 --> 00:02:17,053
A human swimmer is smaller, slower and

37
00:02:17,103 --> 00:02:19,975
weaker than almost
everything on that list.

38
00:02:20,555 --> 00:02:23,185
By any logic, we should be easy prey.

39
00:02:25,881 --> 00:02:30,170
So before we try to answer the question,
let's look at what orcas can do,

40
00:02:30,220 --> 00:02:32,301
when they decide something is food.

41
00:02:38,354 --> 00:02:42,728
In the waters around the Antarctic
Peninsula, scientists from the United

42
00:02:42,778 --> 00:02:47,153
States' National Oceanic and Atmospheric
Administration followed a group

43
00:02:47,203 --> 00:02:50,214
of orcas that hunt seals
resting on floating ice.

44
00:02:50,986 --> 00:02:55,186
First, they rise and look at the
seal, heads above the surface.

45
00:02:55,786 --> 00:02:59,546
Then they back off, line up,
and charge the floe together.

46
00:03:00,046 --> 00:03:02,566
Just before impact, they dive.

47
00:03:03,186 --> 00:03:06,296
The water they push forward
keeps going, as a wave.

48
00:03:07,043 --> 00:03:08,473
If the seal holds on, they do it again.

49
00:03:08,943 --> 00:03:10,013
And again.

50
00:03:10,593 --> 00:03:14,553
And here is the detail
that surprised everyone:

51
00:03:15,173 --> 00:03:19,503
Weddell seals made up only around
fifteen percent of the seals on the ice,

52
00:03:19,553 --> 00:03:23,223
yet fourteen of the fifteen kills
recorded were Weddell seals.

53
00:03:23,908 --> 00:03:25,738
They were not taking whatever they found.

54
00:03:26,318 --> 00:03:27,318
They were choosing.

55
00:03:29,835 --> 00:03:34,631
In Argentine Patagonia, some orcas
ride a wave straight onto the beach,

56
00:03:34,681 --> 00:03:38,025
grab a sea lion pup, and
twist back into the sea.

57
00:03:38,625 --> 00:03:40,465
It is extremely dangerous.

58
00:03:41,075 --> 00:03:44,693
An orca that misjudges the wave can
end up stuck on the sand, where its

59
00:03:44,743 --> 00:03:46,035
own weight could kill it.

60
00:03:46,680 --> 00:03:50,442
A local ranger reported that only
seven orcas there were known to hunt

61
00:03:50,492 --> 00:03:53,760
this way, and only five of them
taught it to younger whales.

62
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:58,329
Young orcas follow an experienced
hunter, stay close to the beach, and

63
00:03:58,379 --> 00:04:00,360
practise without fully committing.

64
00:04:01,057 --> 00:04:06,578
Off Western Australia, in 2019,
researchers watched a dozen or so orcas

65
00:04:06,628 --> 00:04:09,217
chase down and kill a blue whale.

66
00:04:09,787 --> 00:04:12,247
Around fifty orcas then
gathered to share it.

67
00:04:12,747 --> 00:04:18,597
By 2021, the same team had documented
three blue whale kills in the area.

68
00:04:19,237 --> 00:04:23,474
And off South Africa, two male orcas
named Port and Starboard drove great

69
00:04:23,524 --> 00:04:26,577
white sharks away from some
of their favourite bays.

70
00:04:27,187 --> 00:04:32,587
In 2023, researchers watched Starboard,
alone, kill a young great white in

71
00:04:32,637 --> 00:04:34,257
less than two minutes.

72
00:04:34,924 --> 00:04:39,512
Dead great whites washed up on the
beaches, each missing the same organ:

73
00:04:39,562 --> 00:04:41,624
its liver, huge and rich in oil.

74
00:04:42,184 --> 00:04:47,536
On a single day in February 2023, at
least seventeen sevengill sharks were

75
00:04:47,586 --> 00:04:50,944
found dead off the same
coast, livers removed.

76
00:04:51,374 --> 00:04:56,664
In some places, shark cage diving, a
whole local industry, came to a stop.

77
00:04:57,168 --> 00:04:58,628
These are not random attacks.

78
00:04:59,258 --> 00:05:00,558
They are techniques.

79
00:05:01,098 --> 00:05:03,758
Planned, practised, and passed down.

80
00:05:05,447 --> 00:05:09,587
An animal that can hunt a great white
shark could hunt a human in seconds.

81
00:05:10,177 --> 00:05:11,287
So why doesn't it?

82
00:05:18,383 --> 00:05:19,883
Let's look at the evidence.

83
00:05:20,473 --> 00:05:23,703
Every known encounter between
wild orcas and people.

84
00:05:24,343 --> 00:05:29,196
In September 1972, near Point Sur
in California, a surfer named Hans

85
00:05:29,246 --> 00:05:32,203
Kretschmer said he was bitten by an orca.

86
00:05:32,783 --> 00:05:34,723
He needed around a hundred stitches.

87
00:05:35,313 --> 00:05:38,393
Experts think it was a
case of mistaken identity.

88
00:05:38,993 --> 00:05:42,533
He was wearing a black wetsuit,
and sea lions were nearby.

89
00:05:43,191 --> 00:05:45,561
The orca bit, and then let go.

90
00:05:46,141 --> 00:05:47,481
It did not come back.

91
00:05:50,152 --> 00:05:54,944
In 1989, in Patagonia, researcher Bernd
Würsig was standing near the water

92
00:05:54,994 --> 00:05:57,612
when a beaching orca surged towards him.

93
00:05:58,152 --> 00:05:59,782
It missed him by about a metre.

94
00:06:00,342 --> 00:06:02,472
He ran up the beach, unhurt.

95
00:06:03,119 --> 00:06:07,780
In August 2005, near Ketchikan in
Alaska, a twelve-year-old boy named

96
00:06:07,830 --> 00:06:12,696
Ellis Miller was swimming in shallow
water when a transient orca, one of

97
00:06:12,746 --> 00:06:15,819
the seal hunters, bumped
him on the shoulder.

98
00:06:16,369 --> 00:06:17,779
Then it swam away.

99
00:06:18,369 --> 00:06:19,369
He was not bitten.

100
00:06:19,939 --> 00:06:25,118
In 2014, in New Zealand, a freediver
was dragged underwater for more than

101
00:06:25,168 --> 00:06:26,959
forty seconds by an orca.

102
00:06:27,519 --> 00:06:31,489
But the orca had grabbed a bag
tied to his arm, not the man.

103
00:06:31,999 --> 00:06:34,869
An expert called it an
accidental entanglement.

104
00:06:35,460 --> 00:06:39,180
Over many decades, that is about
all the evidence there is.

105
00:06:39,810 --> 00:06:43,130
A handful of bites, bumps and close calls.

106
00:06:43,835 --> 00:06:48,347
The only death ever claimed is an old
story from the Canadian Arctic, told

107
00:06:48,397 --> 00:06:50,689
by Inuit elders: a young man killed in

108
00:06:50,739 --> 00:06:53,205
the 1950s near orcas trapped in the ice.

109
00:06:53,835 --> 00:06:58,133
Researchers could never confirm it,
and some think the cold water was the

110
00:06:58,183 --> 00:06:59,255
more likely cause.

111
00:07:01,895 --> 00:07:03,375
Compare that with sharks.

112
00:07:03,995 --> 00:07:09,719
In 2025 alone, the International
Shark Attack File recorded sixty-five

113
00:07:09,769 --> 00:07:15,295
unprovoked shark bites on people
around the world, and nine deaths.

114
00:07:15,937 --> 00:07:19,907
Orcas are bigger, smarter and
better hunters than any shark.

115
00:07:20,457 --> 00:07:22,087
Some of them hunt sharks.

116
00:07:22,697 --> 00:07:25,337
And yet their record
with us is almost empty.

117
00:07:28,036 --> 00:07:31,106
But there is one place where that
record looks very different.

118
00:07:38,260 --> 00:07:41,110
In captivity, the story changes.

119
00:07:42,845 --> 00:07:44,500
There have been dozens of violent

120
00:07:44,550 --> 00:07:47,185
incidents between captive
orcas and their trainers.

121
00:07:47,805 --> 00:07:49,605
And at least four people have died.

122
00:07:50,347 --> 00:07:54,277
Three of those deaths involved
a single male orca, Tilikum.

123
00:07:54,757 --> 00:07:59,597
The last was the SeaWorld
trainer Dawn Brancheau, in 2010.

124
00:08:00,290 --> 00:08:04,620
Researchers who study wild orcas
point to what captivity takes away.

125
00:08:05,180 --> 00:08:06,979
In the wild, orcas swim dozens

126
00:08:07,029 --> 00:08:09,740
of kilometres every day
with their families.

127
00:08:10,360 --> 00:08:12,183
In a tank, they have neither the

128
00:08:12,233 --> 00:08:14,750
space nor the social
life they evolved for.

129
00:08:15,430 --> 00:08:16,980
The contrast is striking.

130
00:08:17,620 --> 00:08:22,000
Free orcas, with every
opportunity, almost never hurt us.

131
00:08:22,620 --> 00:08:26,740
Confined orcas, under
stress, sometimes do.

132
00:08:29,327 --> 00:08:31,560
So whatever protects us in the wild, it

133
00:08:31,610 --> 00:08:34,537
is not that orcas are
incapable of harming humans.

134
00:08:35,077 --> 00:08:36,377
It is something else.

135
00:08:36,937 --> 00:08:39,667
Something they bring with
them from the open ocean.

136
00:08:42,379 --> 00:08:45,299
And the first clue lies in
what orcas choose to eat.

137
00:08:51,510 --> 00:08:56,425
In the 1970s, a Canadian scientist
named Michael Bigg began studying the

138
00:08:56,475 --> 00:09:01,459
orcas off British Columbia, learning
to recognise each whale by the shape

139
00:09:01,509 --> 00:09:02,750
of its dorsal fin.

140
00:09:03,467 --> 00:09:04,917
He noticed something odd.

141
00:09:05,537 --> 00:09:09,037
In the very same waters, there
were two kinds of orcas.

142
00:09:09,637 --> 00:09:11,027
And they did not mix.

143
00:09:11,742 --> 00:09:14,271
The first kind lived in large, tight

144
00:09:14,321 --> 00:09:17,832
family groups and ate fish,
almost always salmon.

145
00:09:18,352 --> 00:09:20,002
Today they are known as residents.

146
00:09:20,639 --> 00:09:26,079
The second kind roamed in smaller
groups and ignored the salmon entirely.

147
00:09:26,589 --> 00:09:28,359
They hunted seals, sea
lions and other whales.

148
00:09:28,959 --> 00:09:30,489
Today they carry his name:

149
00:09:31,059 --> 00:09:32,389
Bigg's killer whales.

150
00:09:33,102 --> 00:09:36,312
Side by side, they look almost identical.

151
00:09:36,892 --> 00:09:40,629
But genetic studies suggest the two
lines split more than three hundred

152
00:09:40,679 --> 00:09:41,692
thousand years ago.

153
00:09:42,202 --> 00:09:44,872
In 2024, a team of researchers even

154
00:09:44,922 --> 00:09:48,342
proposed that they are
two separate species.

155
00:09:48,922 --> 00:09:51,932
The scientific community
has not fully agreed.

156
00:09:52,509 --> 00:09:54,189
Here is what matters for us.

157
00:09:54,799 --> 00:09:58,439
Each orca population has its
own menu, and it sticks to it.

158
00:09:59,049 --> 00:10:02,129
Whale researcher Erich Hoyt
calls orcas fussy eaters.

159
00:10:02,729 --> 00:10:05,769
They eat what they learn to eat from
their mothers and their families.

160
00:10:06,399 --> 00:10:07,399
Nothing more.

161
00:10:07,786 --> 00:10:11,606
A fish-eating resident orca does
not suddenly decide to try a seal.

162
00:10:12,186 --> 00:10:15,986
And a seal-hunting orca has no
tradition that says a human is food.

163
00:10:17,693 --> 00:10:21,063
To an orca, food is not
everything that moves.

164
00:10:21,623 --> 00:10:24,033
Food is what your family has always eaten.

165
00:10:26,683 --> 00:10:28,943
And that knowledge has
to come from somewhere.

166
00:10:29,523 --> 00:10:33,223
In orca society, it comes
from the oldest females.

167
00:10:39,447 --> 00:10:40,447
But wait.

168
00:10:40,577 --> 00:10:44,117
If orcas only harm what they
eat, the story would be simple.

169
00:10:44,627 --> 00:10:45,627
It isn't.

170
00:10:46,854 --> 00:10:49,574
The salmon-eating Southern
Residents have a strange habit.

171
00:10:50,194 --> 00:10:51,684
They chase porpoises.

172
00:10:52,284 --> 00:10:55,254
They push them, toss them,
sometimes for hours.

173
00:10:55,864 --> 00:10:57,834
And sometimes the porpoise dies.

174
00:10:58,539 --> 00:11:04,312
In 2023, a study led by Deborah Giles,
of the research group Wild Orca,

175
00:11:04,362 --> 00:11:10,053
and Sarah Teman, of the University
of California, Davis, reviewed more

176
00:11:10,103 --> 00:11:12,399
than sixty years of records.

177
00:11:12,999 --> 00:11:17,788
They found seventy-eight documented
cases of Southern Residents harassing

178
00:11:17,838 --> 00:11:19,959
porpoises between 1962 and 2020.

179
00:11:20,648 --> 00:11:21,698
They never eat them.

180
00:11:22,248 --> 00:11:23,988
Porpoises are not on their menu.

181
00:11:25,704 --> 00:11:28,544
The researchers proposed
three explanations.

182
00:11:29,174 --> 00:11:31,684
It could be social play
that helps the group bond.

183
00:11:32,204 --> 00:11:36,184
It could be practice, with a porpoise
standing in for a fast salmon.

184
00:11:36,804 --> 00:11:41,085
Or it could be a kind of misplaced
caregiving, as if the orcas were trying

185
00:11:41,135 --> 00:11:43,184
to look after a small, weak animal.

186
00:11:43,856 --> 00:11:46,516
So orcas can harm animals they do not eat.

187
00:11:47,126 --> 00:11:50,556
Which makes their behaviour around
people even more remarkable.

188
00:11:52,256 --> 00:11:57,116
Humans swim, surf, dive and
sail in orca waters every day.

189
00:11:57,596 --> 00:12:01,606
And still, no wild orca has ever been
confirmed to have killed one of us.

190
00:12:07,753 --> 00:12:09,873
Very few animals go through menopause.

191
00:12:10,453 --> 00:12:11,453
Humans do.

192
00:12:12,003 --> 00:12:14,462
And, as far as we know, only five

193
00:12:14,512 --> 00:12:17,933
other species do: all
of them toothed whales.

194
00:12:18,543 --> 00:12:19,593
Orcas are one of them.

195
00:12:20,264 --> 00:12:23,524
A female orca stops having
calves partway through her life.

196
00:12:24,164 --> 00:12:26,394
But she can live for decades afterwards.

197
00:12:27,059 --> 00:12:30,980
Researchers from the University of
Exeter found part of the answer by

198
00:12:31,030 --> 00:12:34,599
studying the resident orcas of
the North Pacific over decades.

199
00:12:35,334 --> 00:12:39,224
When salmon are scarce, older
post-menopausal females are more likely to

200
00:12:39,274 --> 00:12:42,064
lead the group as it travels
and searches for food.

201
00:12:42,564 --> 00:12:46,542
They seem to carry the knowledge of
where the fish can be found, built up

202
00:12:46,592 --> 00:12:48,964
over a lifetime of good
years and bad ones.

203
00:12:49,687 --> 00:12:53,655
In a 2019 study of almost four
hundred young orcas, calves whose

204
00:12:53,705 --> 00:12:58,175
grandmother had died in the previous
two years had a mortality rate four

205
00:12:58,225 --> 00:13:02,117
and a half times higher than
calves with a living grandmother.

206
00:13:02,805 --> 00:13:06,645
In a society built on knowledge,
the elders are the library.

207
00:13:07,255 --> 00:13:10,885
They teach where to hunt, how
to hunt, and what to hunt.

208
00:13:11,493 --> 00:13:14,003
And, perhaps, what to leave alone.

209
00:13:16,717 --> 00:13:20,615
Because if hunting traditions can be
passed down for generations, so can

210
00:13:20,665 --> 00:13:21,707
the absence of one.

211
00:13:22,307 --> 00:13:25,587
No grandmother has ever taught
her family that humans are prey.

212
00:13:31,801 --> 00:13:33,161
There may be another reason.

213
00:13:33,711 --> 00:13:35,401
Orcas are not just strong.

214
00:13:35,961 --> 00:13:36,961
They are careful.

215
00:13:37,441 --> 00:13:41,791
They have one of the largest brains in
the ocean, and they live in complex

216
00:13:41,841 --> 00:13:45,051
societies, with family bonds
that can last a lifetime.

217
00:13:45,623 --> 00:13:51,792
On July twenty-fourth, 2018, off the
coast of British Columbia, a female

218
00:13:51,842 --> 00:13:57,283
resident orca known as Tahlequah,
or J35, gave birth to a calf.

219
00:13:57,943 --> 00:14:00,283
The calf died within about half an hour.

220
00:14:00,917 --> 00:14:02,217
She did not let go.

221
00:14:02,777 --> 00:14:06,562
She carried the body for about seventeen
days, over roughly one thousand

222
00:14:06,612 --> 00:14:07,837
six hundred kilometres.

223
00:14:08,447 --> 00:14:11,087
Other members of her pod
were seen taking turns.

224
00:14:11,794 --> 00:14:15,434
At the very end of 2024, she did it again.

225
00:14:16,044 --> 00:14:21,174
A new calf, a female, died within
days, and Tahlequah was seen carrying

226
00:14:21,224 --> 00:14:23,194
her body into the new year.

227
00:14:24,814 --> 00:14:26,954
We cannot know what an orca feels.

228
00:14:27,574 --> 00:14:31,254
But an animal that behaves like this
is not a simple hunting machine.

229
00:14:31,844 --> 00:14:35,714
It reacts to its world with
something we struggle to name.

230
00:14:38,353 --> 00:14:42,263
Hoyt says wild orcas are generally
careful around people underwater.

231
00:14:42,867 --> 00:14:47,754
To an animal like this, a strange,
slow, upright creature in the water may

232
00:14:47,804 --> 00:14:51,607
simply be something to
investigate, not something to eat.

233
00:14:57,780 --> 00:15:02,035
For thousands of years, people along
the coast of the North Pacific have

234
00:15:02,085 --> 00:15:03,400
lived alongside orcas.

235
00:15:03,980 --> 00:15:06,980
And some of their stories
offer an answer of their own.

236
00:15:07,710 --> 00:15:11,903
One Tlingit story tells of Natsilane,
a wood carver who was abandoned at

237
00:15:11,953 --> 00:15:13,250
sea by his own family.

238
00:15:13,830 --> 00:15:17,813
He carved a killer whale from wood,
and it came to life and took revenge

239
00:15:17,863 --> 00:15:19,320
on those who betrayed him.

240
00:15:19,928 --> 00:15:22,398
Afterwards, Natsilane
regretted the killing.

241
00:15:22,978 --> 00:15:26,208
And he told the whale that it
must never harm a human again.

242
00:15:28,828 --> 00:15:29,878
It is a legend, not science.

243
00:15:30,168 --> 00:15:34,868
But it says something people noticed
long before scientists did: the

244
00:15:34,918 --> 00:15:39,458
ocean's greatest hunter seems to
have an unwritten rule about us.

245
00:15:42,151 --> 00:15:44,275
And in the last few years, one group

246
00:15:44,325 --> 00:15:46,681
of orcas has put that rule to the test.

247
00:15:52,839 --> 00:15:58,165
Since 2020, sailors off the coasts of
Spain and Portugal have reported a

248
00:15:58,215 --> 00:15:59,559
strange encounter.

249
00:16:00,149 --> 00:16:04,545
A group of orcas approaches a sailboat,
goes straight for the rudder, and

250
00:16:04,595 --> 00:16:06,239
pushes, bites and spins it.

251
00:16:06,866 --> 00:16:09,576
More than seven hundred
interactions have been logged.

252
00:16:10,116 --> 00:16:11,786
At least eight boats have sunk.

253
00:16:12,418 --> 00:16:14,562
Online, the story became a revenge

254
00:16:14,612 --> 00:16:17,258
tale: orcas fighting back against humans.

255
00:16:17,878 --> 00:16:21,228
Most scientists studying them
do not support that idea.

256
00:16:21,818 --> 00:16:25,876
Young orcas seem to copy the behaviour
from older ones, the way a game

257
00:16:25,926 --> 00:16:27,628
spreads through a playground.

258
00:16:28,266 --> 00:16:29,786
But look at what they target.

259
00:16:30,376 --> 00:16:31,376
The rudder.

260
00:16:31,586 --> 00:16:32,586
Not the people.

261
00:16:34,210 --> 00:16:38,425
Across hundreds of encounters, with
crews standing on deck just a few

262
00:16:38,475 --> 00:16:41,690
metres away, no one has been
injured by these orcas.

263
00:16:44,416 --> 00:16:48,661
Even when orcas develop a brand-new
habit that involves humans, they still

264
00:16:48,711 --> 00:16:50,046
leave the humans alone.

265
00:16:56,124 --> 00:16:59,044
So the answer to our
question is not one thing.

266
00:16:59,624 --> 00:17:00,864
It is a combination.

267
00:17:01,444 --> 00:17:02,844
We are not on the menu.

268
00:17:03,414 --> 00:17:04,934
The elders never taught it.

269
00:17:05,434 --> 00:17:07,912
And orcas are careful,
curious animals that

270
00:17:07,962 --> 00:17:10,314
rarely mistake us for anything they eat.

271
00:17:10,904 --> 00:17:12,974
But there is a darker side to this story.

272
00:17:13,484 --> 00:17:17,704
Because if anything in this
relationship is dangerous, it is us.

273
00:17:20,409 --> 00:17:24,527
The Southern Residents, a community
of fish-eating orcas off Washington

274
00:17:24,577 --> 00:17:27,689
State and British Columbia,
depend on Chinook salmon.

275
00:17:28,239 --> 00:17:30,079
And Chinook salmon have declined.

276
00:17:30,766 --> 00:17:34,816
In 1995, there were ninety-eight
Southern Residents.

277
00:17:35,386 --> 00:17:39,156
By the end of 2021, there
were seventy-three.

278
00:17:39,766 --> 00:17:45,981
The 2026 census counted seventy-six,
and in October 2026, a new calf was

279
00:17:46,031 --> 00:17:49,946
spotted, bringing the
count to seventy-seven.

280
00:17:50,652 --> 00:17:54,858
The Iberian orcas, the ones spinning
rudders, number fewer than fifty

281
00:17:54,908 --> 00:17:57,992
animals, and are considered
critically endangered.

282
00:17:58,557 --> 00:18:02,317
The animals that refuse to hunt us are
the ones that need our protection.

283
00:18:08,536 --> 00:18:10,756
We gave them a frightening
name: killer whale.

284
00:18:11,436 --> 00:18:12,636
And they are killers.

285
00:18:13,246 --> 00:18:15,736
Perhaps the most skilled
hunters in the ocean.

286
00:18:16,226 --> 00:18:21,086
They can take down a great white shark,
a blue whale, a seal on the ice.

287
00:18:21,767 --> 00:18:23,867
But when they meet us, they look.

288
00:18:24,457 --> 00:18:25,457
They wonder.

289
00:18:25,847 --> 00:18:27,057
And they move on.

290
00:18:29,730 --> 00:18:31,010
Not because they can't.

291
00:18:31,470 --> 00:18:33,180
But because, as far as we can

292
00:18:33,230 --> 00:18:35,960
tell, nothing in their
culture tells them to.

293
00:18:40,657 --> 00:18:42,959
If you enjoyed this film, subscribe to

294
00:18:43,009 --> 00:18:45,857
Naturexplore for a new
documentary every week.

295
00:18:46,447 --> 00:18:50,077
And tell us in the comments: why
do you think orcas leave us alone?

